sections.statement:
From an inertial reference frame outside Earth, the observer describes a circle parallel to the ecliptic, with constant speed and a period of one sidereal day. Since the ecliptic poles have zero altitude, this is a great circle.

The resulting movement is the observer's movement relative to Earth's surface plus Earth's movement relative to the inertial frame. After time $t$, the observer describes an arc $\varphi=2\pi/T$ on the great circle while Earth rotates through the same amount. The displacement is the composition of a rotation by $\varphi$ about the ecliptic axis and a rotation by $-\varphi$ about Earth's rotation axis. This is the construction of an analemma for a planet with a circular orbit and inclination $\varepsilon=23.44^\circ$ between ecliptic and equator.

The curve:

- is closed;
- has maximum and minimum latitudes $\theta=\pm23.44^\circ$;
- is symmetric about the equator;
- is symmetric about a meridian;
- has the shape of an eight.

Starting in the southern hemisphere, the observer first crosses the equator south to north. In the inertial frame the speed is $v=2\pi R/T$ and azimuth $\alpha=90^\circ-\varepsilon$, measured from north–south. Earth's surface has the same speed and azimuth $90^\circ$. Subtracting the vectors gives

$$v'=v\sqrt{2(1-\cos\varepsilon)},\qquad\alpha'=-\frac\varepsilon2=-11.72^\circ.$$

**Marking:** 1 pt for each curve characteristic; velocity 3 pt; direction 2 pt.

parts.statement:
Оценете броя на звездите в галактиката, приемайки,че:

- средната маса на звездите е равна на масата на Слънцето и една трета от барионната материя на галактиката е под формата на звезди;
- отношението на барионната към тъмната материя в галактиката е същото каквото е това отношение за цялата Вселена (вижте таблицата с константите).
